        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "A problem well-put is half solved.\n—JOHN DEWEY",
            "block_bbox": [
                296,
                370,
                574,
                424
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 1,
            "global_block_id": 1,
            "global_group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    296.0,
                    370.0
                ],
                [
                    574.0,
                    370.0
                ],
                [
                    574.0,
                    424.0
                ],
                [
                    296.0,
                    424.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The principles discussed in Part Two apply to sentences and short passages in all kinds of writing. We now turn in Part Three to principles that apply to whole documents and sections of documents. While these principles are relevant to most kinds of expository and argumentative writing, our focus will be on how they apply to the kinds of writing people do as students, as scholars and researchers, and on the job.",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                450,
                784,
                578
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "global_block_id": 2,
            "global_group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    450.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    450.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    578.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    578.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Of course, the various genres of academic and professional writing—critical essays, research reports, legal briefs, business proposals, even op-eds—differ from one another in many ways. But they also share features in common, features readers rely on to help them follow and make sense of what they read.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                580,
                784,
                682
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "global_block_id": 3,
            "global_group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    580.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    580.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    682.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    682.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "This lesson addresses two of the most important of these: introductions and conclusions. Lesson 7 explains things you can do throughout a piece of writing to lead readers to experience it as organized and coherent. In both lessons, we take examples mainly from academic genres, but the advice and principles apply to professional genres as well.",
            "block_bbox": [
                89,
                684,
                784,
                813
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "global_block_id": 4,
            "global_group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    89.0,
                    684.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    684.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    813.0
                ],
                [
                    89.0,
                    813.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "Framing Introductions to Help Readers",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                844,
                586,
                875
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 5,
            "global_block_id": 5,
            "global_group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    844.0
                ],
                [
                    586.0,
                    844.0
                ],
                [
                    586.0,
                    875.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    875.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Writers working in academic and professional contexts generally want to inform or persuade readers who know less than they do, which means they are vulnerable to thinking their writing is clear when their readers won't. Also, such writers can't assume their readers are already deeply invested in what they have to say. Accordingly, when you write in these contexts, you face a double challenge:",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                888,
                783,
                1017
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 6,
            "global_block_id": 6,
            "global_group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    888.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    888.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    1017.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    1017.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- to let readers know what to expect so that they can read more knowledgeably",
            "block_bbox": [
                129,
                1030,
                782,
                1080
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 7,
            "global_block_id": 7,
            "global_group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    129.0,
                    1030.0
                ],
                [
                    782.0,
                    1030.0
                ],
                [
                    782.0,
                    1080.0
                ],
                [
                    129.0,
                    1080.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "• to motivate readers so that they want to read carefully",
            "block_bbox": [
                129,
                1088,
                610,
                1112
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 8,
            "global_block_id": 8,
            "global_group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    129.0,
                    1088.0
                ],
                [
                    610.0,
                    1088.0
                ],
                [
                    610.0,
                    1112.0
                ],
                [
                    129.0,
                    1112.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "You do these things best when you write not just about a topic that interests you but about a problem that is important to your readers. If they don't yet care—or even know—about that problem, you have to show them why they should.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                1126,
                784,
                1203
            ],
